/*
------------------------------------------------------------
Problem    : Word Search
Platform   : LeetCode
Topic      : Matrix
Difficulty : Medium

Problem:
Given an m x n grid of characters and a string word, return
true if the word exists in the grid.

The word can be constructed using sequentially adjacent
cells.

A cell can be connected to another cell only if it is:
- Horizontally adjacent
- Vertically adjacent

The same cell cannot be used more than once in the same path.

Example:

    board = [
        ['A','B','C','E'],
        ['S','F','C','S'],
        ['A','D','E','E']
    ]

    word = "ABCCED"

Output:

    true

Approach:
Use Depth First Search (DFS) with Backtracking.

We try every cell as a possible starting point.

For each cell:
1. Check whether the current character matches word[index].
2. If it matches, temporarily mark the cell as visited.
3. Explore all four possible directions:
      Down
      Up
      Right
      Left
4. If any direction successfully finds the remaining word,
   return true.
5. Restore the original character before returning.

Why do we mark the cell?

The same cell cannot be used more than once in the same path.

For example, when processing:

    A -> B -> C -> C -> E -> D

we must make sure that we do not use a previously visited
cell again.

We temporarily replace the character with '#'.

After exploring the path, we restore the original character.
This is the backtracking step.

Base Case:

    if(index == word.size())

This means that every character of the word has been matched,
so we return true.

Boundary / Invalid Case:

If:
- i or j is outside the board
- current cell does not match word[index]

then this path cannot work, so return false.

Example:

    word = "ABCCED"

Starting from 'A':

    A
    ↓
    B
    ↓
    C
    ↓
    C
    ↓
    E
    ↓
    D

The complete word is found, so return true.

Backtracking:

Suppose we reach a wrong path:

    A -> B -> C -> B

There is no valid next cell for the required character.

We return to the previous cell and try another direction.

This process continues until:
- The complete word is found, or
- Every possible path has been explored.

Algorithm:
1. Traverse every cell in the board.
2. If the cell can be the starting point of the word,
   start DFS.
3. Match the current character.
4. Mark the current cell as visited.
5. Explore the four neighboring cells.
6. Restore the cell after the recursive calls.
7. Return true if any path finds the complete word.
8. Otherwise return false.

Time Complexity : O(m * n * 4^L)

Where:
    m = number of rows
    n = number of columns
    L = length of the word

For every starting cell, DFS can explore up to four
directions at each step.

A tighter bound is often written as:

    O(m * n * 3^L)

because after the first move, we cannot immediately move
back to the cell we just visited.

Space Complexity : O(L)

Reason:
The maximum recursion depth is equal to the length of
the word.

The board itself is modified temporarily, so no separate
visited matrix is required.

------------------------------------------------------------
*/

class Solution {
private:

    bool dfs(
        vector<vector<char>>& board,
        string& word,
        int i,
        int j,
        int index
    ) {

        // All characters have been matched
        if (index == word.size()) {
            return true;
        }

        // Invalid position or character mismatch
        if (
            i < 0 ||
            j < 0 ||
            i >= board.size() ||
            j >= board[0].size() ||
            board[i][j] != word[index]
        ) {
            return false;
        }

        // Mark current cell as visited
        char temp = board[i][j];
        board[i][j] = '#';

        // Explore all four directions
        bool found =
            dfs(board, word, i + 1, j, index + 1) || // Down
            dfs(board, word, i - 1, j, index + 1) || // Up
            dfs(board, word, i, j + 1, index + 1) || // Right
            dfs(board, word, i, j - 1, index + 1);   // Left

        // Backtrack: restore original character
        board[i][j] = temp;

        return found;
    }

public:

    bool exist(vector<vector<char>>& board, string word) {

        int m = board.size();
        int n = board[0].size();

        // Try every cell as a starting point
        for (int i = 0; i < m; i++) {
            for (int j = 0; j < n; j++) {

                if (dfs(board, word, i, j, 0)) {
                    return true;
                }
            }
        }

        return false;
    }
};
